{"type":"text","timestamp":1793094542330,"sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","part":{"id":"prt_17cc3597e001j3A0QgGJJQ9MsZ","sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","messageID":"msg_17cc31d860015uQUz3uSRnhckJ","type":"text","text":"The sibling lineage gives me exact shapes to follow. Let me examine its artifacts in detail.\n\n","time":{"start":1793094539646,"end":1793094542330}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793094542347,"sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","part":{"partID":"prt_17cc36064001Ups6Be44iTxw30","sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","messageID":"msg_17cc31d860015uQUz3uSRnhckJ","type":"tool","id":"call_221ef9d9cc321ef1d2e3312e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":100,"path":"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-001.md"},"output":"Read file specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-001.md, lines 1-95\n1: # Iteration 1: The sk-git/026 regression — who moved the worktrees back and where the reaper's daemon handling went\n2: \n3: ## Focus\n4: \n5: Q1 of the packet's section 8: what unset `speckit.worktreeBase` and removed the reaper's daemon handling, and whether anything still defaults worktrees to `.worktrees/`. This iteration reads the completed sk-git/026 packet, the current worktree scripts, and the git history of the reaper, and re-measures the live daemon population.\n6: \n7: ## Actions Taken\n8: \n9: 1. Read `specs/sk-git/026-worktree-hygiene-and-relocation/implementation-summary.md` for the recorded outcomes (prune, fsmonitor on, configurable base set to `~/worktrees/public`, reaper \"generalized daemon-orphan detection\").\n10: 2. Read the resolved-base code paths: `.skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh` `_wn_base_dir()`, `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh` base resolution and config write-back, `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh` header and section list.\n11: 3. Traced the reaper's history with `git log --oneline -- <path>` and `git log -S \"daemon\"` over both the pre-move `.opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh` and the current `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`, then read the full reaper diff of the commit that dropped the daemon block.\n12: 4. Traced the config write-back history with `git log -S 'config speckit.worktreeBase \"$WT_BASE_DIR\"'`.\n13: 5. Re-measured the live state: `git config --show-origin --get-regexp 'fsmonitor|untracked|worktreeBase|extensions'`, `ls -d .worktrees/*`, `ls ~/worktrees`, and a `ps -Ao pid,lstart,command` census of `fsmonitor--daemon run` processes.\n14: 6. Checked `~/.zsh_history` for an `unset speckit.worktreeBase` record (no hit).\n15: \n16: ## Findings\n17: \n18: 1. The reaper's daemon handling was deleted by `3c7c3f83874` (2026-09-03, \"refactor(runtime): remove the memory launcher, plugin, hooks and the tool-ownership commit gate\"), not by the `.opencode` → `.skilled` source-root move. That commit removed the `--reap-daemons` flag, the `_daemon_worktree_path_from_cmdline()` and `_daemon_cmdline_is_orphan()` helpers, the whole \"5. ORPHAN DAEMON REPORTING\" block, and the closing `orphan daemon candidates` log line from `.opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`. [SOURCE: git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh]\n19: \n20: 2. The deleted logic never matched `git fsmonitor--daemon` at all. It enumerated `pgrep -f 'context-server\\.js'` and skipped every process whose command line was not `*node*context-server.js*`, i.e. the retired spec-memory MCP daemons whose lease lived under a worktree DB dir. Two separate daemon populations were conflated in the packet's premise: sk-git/026 reaped MCP context-server daemons, and today's pile-up is git's own fsmonitor daemon. [SOURCE: git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh]\n21: \n22: 3. Restoring \"the reaper's daemon handling\" therefore would not stop a single fsmonitor daemon. A fsmonitor reaper is new work, not a revert. [INFERENCE: from findings 1-2 plus `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh` now containing no `daemon`, `fsmonitor` or `orphan` token]\n23: \n24: 4. `speckit.worktreeBase` is local-only configuration: `git config --show-origin --get-regexp` prints it nowhere for this checkout (only `file:.git/config core.fsmonitor true` and `core.untrackedcache true`), and no commit can unset an unversioned `.git/config` key. The regression was not a code revert of the relocation policy; the policy record simply is not in the repository. [SOURCE: git config --show-origin output, this session]\n25: \n26: 5. The durable in-checkout default is re-created by the launch wrapper itself. `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292-295` writes `speckit.worktreeBase = $WT_BASE_DIR` whenever the key is unset, and `_wn_base_dir()` falls back to `<toplevel>/.worktrees` when neither `SPECKIT_WORKTREE_BASE` nor the config key exists. So one launch without the env var converts the intended `~/worktrees/public` policy into a permanent `.worktrees` policy. [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:71]\n27: \n28: 6. The auto-pin is recent: `f83952c3a94` (2026-09-11, \"fix(sk-git): keep the reaper off live sessions and record autosync rewrites\") introduced the config write-back, three weeks after `a69a621a39a` (2026-08-21) introduced the configurable base. The window between the two commits is exactly when a launch could have re-pinned `.worktrees` without any operator intent. [SOURCE: git log -S 'config speckit.worktreeBase \"$WT_BASE_DIR\"' -- .opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh]\n29: \n30: 7. Nothing else in the repo opts this checkout out of the in-checkout default. `.worktrees` is still the documented default in `worktree-naming.sh:13`, `README.md:115`, and `README.md:125`, and the only documented way back out is a manual `git config speckit.worktreeBase <abs>`; the changelog even advertises \"Unset the config to return to the in-checkout `.worktrees` layout\" as a supported action. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:13] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/changelog/v1.5.2.0.md:29]\n31: \n32: 8. The live state matches the in-checkout default: `git config --get speckit.worktreeBase` is empty, `~/worktrees` exists but is empty, and 13 worktrees sit under `Public/.worktrees/` (087, 103, 140, 144, 145, 156, 197, 216, 217, 218, 219, 230, 231). The empty `~/worktrees` directory is the residue of the relocation, not of a live policy. [SOURCE: ls -d .worktrees/*, this session] [SOURCE: ls -la ~/worktrees, this session]\n33: \n34: 9. Eleven `git fsmonitor--daemon run --detach --ipc-threads=8` processes are alive as of 09:22Z, the oldest started Oct 11 18:29:55 (PID 1157, 16 days), and every one of them is Xcode's `git-core`. Nothing in the repo starts, stops, or bounds them: the reaper is a manual CLI, and its published invariant is now \"never signals a process\". [SOURCE: ps -Ao pid,lstart,command output, this session] [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/README.md:168]\n35: \n36: 10. Because the reaper documents \"never signals a process\", re-adding daemon killing inside `worktree-reaper.sh` would break a published contract rather than restore one; a fsmonitor reap belongs in its own entry point (or in a `--reap-fsmonitor` sibling) whose invariant can be stated separately. [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/README.md:168] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md:85]\n37: \n38: ## Questions Answered\n39: \n40: - Q1. What unset speckit.worktreeBase and removed the reaper's daemon handling, and does anything still create worktrees under .worktrees/ by default? — Answer: two different events. The reaper's daemon block was deleted by `3c7c3f83874` (2026-09-03) in the memory-launcher refactor, and it only ever targeted MCP `context-server.js` daemons. The base key was not unset by any commit (it is unversioned local config); the launch wrapper re-pins it on any unset launch, defaulting to `.worktrees`. Yes — `.worktrees` remains the code default, all 13 worktrees live there, and 11 fsmonitor daemons persist from Oct 11 onward.\n41: \n42: ## Questions Remaining\n43: \n44: - Q2. Git 2.50.1's documented daemon lifecycle on macOS (idle exit, `fsmonitor.socketDir`, `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` on a wedged daemon).\n45: - Q3. Event overload versus a known 2.50.1 daemon deadlock (`--ipc-threads`, cookie handshake).\n46: - Q4. Which hook/script git call sites lack a timeout.\n47: - Q5. Safest default shape.\n48: \n49: ## Ruled Out\n50: \n51: - \"The `.opencode` → `.skilled` move removed the daemon handling.\" Ruled out: `git log -S daemon` puts the deletion in `3c7c3f83874`, and the move commit `330427cf308` is the only commit touching the current path.\n52: - \"A repo commit reverted the relocated worktree base.\" Ruled out: the key is local config; the only repo-side actor is the wrapper's write-back.\n53: - \"An operator shell command unset it and is recoverable from this shell's history.\" No hit in `~/.zsh_history`; the unset, if manual, happened in another session or before history rotation.\n54: \n55: ## Dead Ends\n56: \n57: - Git-config provenance for `speckit.worktreeBase`: `.git/config` is not versioned, so the exact unset action is unrecoverable from the repository. Recorded as an explicit UNKNOWN rather than guessed.\n58: \n59: ## Edge Cases\n60: \n61: - Contradictory evidence: the packet's own wording calls the removed code \"the reaper's daemon handling\" and the current pile-up \"fsmonitor daemons\", which reads as one lineage of work. The code shows two unrelated daemon families. Resolution: recorded as findings 1-3 rather than smoothed over; the premise correction is load-bearing for the fix design.\n62: - Missing dependency: no access to the pre-unset `.git/config`; the UNKNOWN stands with the check that would resolve it (a `.git/config` copy in a backup or another clone's config).\n63: \n64: ## Sources Consulted\n65: \n66: - `specs/sk-git/026-worktree-hygiene-and-relocation/implementation-summary.md`\n67: - `git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`\n68: - `git log --oneline -S \"daemon\" -- .skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`\n69: - `git log --oneline -S 'config speckit.worktreeBase \"$WT_BASE_DIR\"' -- .opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh`\n70: - `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:219,292-295`\n71: - `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`\n72: - `.skilled/bin/README.md:168`\n73: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:13,61-83`\n74: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/README.md:115,125`\n75: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/changelog/v1.5.2.0.md:21,29`\n76: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md:85`\n77: - `ps -Ao pid,lstart,command | grep [f]smonitor--daemon run` (11 processes)\n78: - `git config --show-origin --get-regexp \"fsmonitor|untracked|worktreeBase|extensions\"`\n79: - `ls -d .worktrees/*`, `ls -la ~/worktrees`\n80: \n81: ## Assessment\n82: \n83: - New information ratio: 0.85\n84: - Questions addressed: Q1\n85: - Questions answered: Q1\n86: \n87: ## Reflection\n88: \n89: - What worked and why: `git log -S` over both path spellings, then reading the full diff of the one commit that matched, converted a vague \"the reaper lost something\" into an exact deletion with a date and a reason. Re-measuring the live process census in the same session kept the fourteen-day-old daemon observation current instead of inherited.\n90: - What did not work and why: chasing config provenance through git history was structurally impossible — the key is unversioned. Reading the wrapper's write-back found the real persistence mechanism instead.\n91: - What I would do differently: start from the code path that writes the key (write-back) rather than from \"who unset it\", because an unset that is silently re-pinned is a different bug from an unset that stays unset.\n92: \n93: ## Recommended Next Focus\n94: \n95: Q2 and Q3 together: git 2.50.1's fsmonitor daemon lifecycle on macOS — whether a daemon can exit on its own, what `fsmonitor.socketDir`, idle shutdown and `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` do, and whether `--ipc-threads=8` or the cookie handshake has a known wedge in this version. The `--ipc-threads=8` argument in every live process is not something this repo passes, so its origin and its deadlock surface need an authoritative answer.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-001.md, lines 1-95\n1: # Iteration 1: The sk-git/026 regression — who moved the worktrees back and where the reaper's daemon handling went\n2: \n3: ## Focus\n4: \n5: Q1 of the packet's section 8: what unset `speckit.worktreeBase` and removed the reaper's daemon handling, and whether anything still defaults worktrees to `.worktrees/`. This iteration reads the completed sk-git/026 packet, the current worktree scripts, and the git history of the reaper, and re-measures the live daemon population.\n6: \n7: ## Actions Taken\n8: \n9: 1. Read `specs/sk-git/026-worktree-hygiene-and-relocation/implementation-summary.md` for the recorded outcomes (prune, fsmonitor on, configurable base set to `~/worktrees/public`, reaper \"generalized daemon-orphan detection\").\n10: 2. Read the resolved-base code paths: `.skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh` `_wn_base_dir()`, `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh` base resolution and config write-back, `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh` header and section list.\n11: 3. Traced the reaper's history with `git log --oneline -- <path>` and `git log -S \"daemon\"` over both the pre-move `.opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh` and the current `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`, then read the full reaper diff of the commit that dropped the daemon block.\n12: 4. Traced the config write-back history with `git log -S 'config speckit.worktreeBase \"$WT_BASE_DIR\"'`.\n13: 5. Re-measured the live state: `git config --show-origin --get-regexp 'fsmonitor|untracked|worktreeBase|extensions'`, `ls -d .worktrees/*`, `ls ~/worktrees`, and a `ps -Ao pid,lstart,command` census of `fsmonitor--daemon run` processes.\n14: 6. Checked `~/.zsh_history` for an `unset speckit.worktreeBase` record (no hit).\n15: \n16: ## Findings\n17: \n18: 1. The reaper's daemon handling was deleted by `3c7c3f83874` (2026-09-03, \"refactor(runtime): remove the memory launcher, plugin, hooks and the tool-ownership commit gate\"), not by the `.opencode` → `.skilled` source-root move. That commit removed the `--reap-daemons` flag, the `_daemon_worktree_path_from_cmdline()` and `_daemon_cmdline_is_orphan()` helpers, the whole \"5. ORPHAN DAEMON REPORTING\" block, and the closing `orphan daemon candidates` log line from `.opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`. [SOURCE: git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh]\n19: \n20: 2. The deleted logic never matched `git fsmonitor--daemon` at all. It enumerated `pgrep -f 'context-server\\.js'` and skipped every process whose command line was not `*node*context-server.js*`, i.e. the retired spec-memory MCP daemons whose lease lived under a worktree DB dir. Two separate daemon populations were conflated in the packet's premise: sk-git/026 reaped MCP context-server daemons, and today's pile-up is git's own fsmonitor daemon. [SOURCE: git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh]\n21: \n22: 3. Restoring \"the reaper's daemon handling\" therefore would not stop a single fsmonitor daemon. A fsmonitor reaper is new work, not a revert. [INFERENCE: from findings 1-2 plus `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh` now containing no `daemon`, `fsmonitor` or `orphan` token]\n23: \n24: 4. `speckit.worktreeBase` is local-only configuration: `git config --show-origin --get-regexp` prints it nowhere for this checkout (only `file:.git/config core.fsmonitor true` and `core.untrackedcache true`), and no commit can unset an unversioned `.git/config` key. The regression was not a code revert of the relocation policy; the policy record simply is not in the repository. [SOURCE: git config --show-origin output, this session]\n25: \n26: 5. The durable in-checkout default is re-created by the launch wrapper itself. `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292-295` writes `speckit.worktreeBase = $WT_BASE_DIR` whenever the key is unset, and `_wn_base_dir()` falls back to `<toplevel>/.worktrees` when neither `SPECKIT_WORKTREE_BASE` nor the config key exists. So one launch without the env var converts the intended `~/worktrees/public` policy into a permanent `.worktrees` policy. [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:71]\n27: \n28: 6. The auto-pin is recent: `f83952c3a94` (2026-09-11, \"fix(sk-git): keep the reaper off live sessions and record autosync rewrites\") introduced the config write-back, three weeks after `a69a621a39a` (2026-08-21) introduced the configurable base. The window between the two commits is exactly when a launch could have re-pinned `.worktrees` without any operator intent. [SOURCE: git log -S 'config speckit.worktreeBase \"$WT_BASE_DIR\"' -- .opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh]\n29: \n30: 7. Nothing else in the repo opts this checkout out of the in-checkout default. `.worktrees` is still the documented default in `worktree-naming.sh:13`, `README.md:115`, and `README.md:125`, and the only documented way back out is a manual `git config speckit.worktreeBase <abs>`; the changelog even advertises \"Unset the config to return to the in-checkout `.worktrees` layout\" as a supported action. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:13] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/changelog/v1.5.2.0.md:29]\n31: \n32: 8. The live state matches the in-checkout default: `git config --get speckit.worktreeBase` is empty, `~/worktrees` exists but is empty, and 13 worktrees sit under `Public/.worktrees/` (087, 103, 140, 144, 145, 156, 197, 216, 217, 218, 219, 230, 231). The empty `~/worktrees` directory is the residue of the relocation, not of a live policy. [SOURCE: ls -d .worktrees/*, this session] [SOURCE: ls -la ~/worktrees, this session]\n33: \n34: 9. Eleven `git fsmonitor--daemon run --detach --ipc-threads=8` processes are alive as of 09:22Z, the oldest started Oct 11 18:29:55 (PID 1157, 16 days), and every one of them is Xcode's `git-core`. Nothing in the repo starts, stops, or bounds them: the reaper is a manual CLI, and its published invariant is now \"never signals a process\". [SOURCE: ps -Ao pid,lstart,command output, this session] [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/README.md:168]\n35: \n36: 10. Because the reaper documents \"never signals a process\", re-adding daemon killing inside `worktree-reaper.sh` would break a published contract rather than restore one; a fsmonitor reap belongs in its own entry point (or in a `--reap-fsmonitor` sibling) whose invariant can be stated separately. [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/README.md:168] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md:85]\n37: \n38: ## Questions Answered\n39: \n40: - Q1. What unset speckit.worktreeBase and removed the reaper's daemon handling, and does anything still create worktrees under .worktrees/ by default? — Answer: two different events. The reaper's daemon block was deleted by `3c7c3f83874` (2026-09-03) in the memory-launcher refactor, and it only ever targeted MCP `context-server.js` daemons. The base key was not unset by any commit (it is unversioned local config); the launch wrapper re-pins it on any unset launch, defaulting to `.worktrees`. Yes — `.worktrees` remains the code default, all 13 worktrees live there, and 11 fsmonitor daemons persist from Oct 11 onward.\n41: \n42: ## Questions Remaining\n43: \n44: - Q2. Git 2.50.1's documented daemon lifecycle on macOS (idle exit, `fsmonitor.socketDir`, `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` on a wedged daemon).\n45: - Q3. Event overload versus a known 2.50.1 daemon deadlock (`--ipc-threads`, cookie handshake).\n46: - Q4. Which hook/script git call sites lack a timeout.\n47: - Q5. Safest default shape.\n48: \n49: ## Ruled Out\n50: \n51: - \"The `.opencode` → `.skilled` move removed the daemon handling.\" Ruled out: `git log -S daemon` puts the deletion in `3c7c3f83874`, and the move commit `330427cf308` is the only commit touching the current path.\n52: - \"A repo commit reverted the relocated worktree base.\" Ruled out: the key is local config; the only repo-side actor is the wrapper's write-back.\n53: - \"An operator shell command unset it and is recoverable from this shell's history.\" No hit in `~/.zsh_history`; the unset, if manual, happened in another session or before history rotation.\n54: \n55: ## Dead Ends\n56: \n57: - Git-config provenance for `speckit.worktreeBase`: `.git/config` is not versioned, so the exact unset action is unrecoverable from the repository. Recorded as an explicit UNKNOWN rather than guessed.\n58: \n59: ## Edge Cases\n60: \n61: - Contradictory evidence: the packet's own wording calls the removed code \"the reaper's daemon handling\" and the current pile-up \"fsmonitor daemons\", which reads as one lineage of work. The code shows two unrelated daemon families. Resolution: recorded as findings 1-3 rather than smoothed over; the premise correction is load-bearing for the fix design.\n62: - Missing dependency: no access to the pre-unset `.git/config`; the UNKNOWN stands with the check that would resolve it (a `.git/config` copy in a backup or another clone's config).\n63: \n64: ## Sources Consulted\n65: \n66: - `specs/sk-git/026-worktree-hygiene-and-relocation/implementation-summary.md`\n67: - `git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`\n68: - `git log --oneline -S \"daemon\" -- .skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`\n69: - `git log --oneline -S 'config speckit.worktreeBase \"$WT_BASE_DIR\"' -- .opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh`\n70: - `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:219,292-295`\n71: - `.skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh`\n72: - `.skilled/bin/README.md:168`\n73: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:13,61-83`\n74: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/README.md:115,125`\n75: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/changelog/v1.5.2.0.md:21,29`\n76: - `.skilled/skills/sk-git/feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md:85`\n77: - `ps -Ao pid,lstart,command | grep [f]smonitor--daemon run` (11 processes)\n78: - `git config --show-origin --get-regexp \"fsmonitor|untracked|worktreeBase|extensions\"`\n79: - `ls -d .worktrees/*`, `ls -la ~/worktrees`\n80: \n81: ## Assessment\n82: \n83: - New information ratio: 0.85\n84: - Questions addressed: Q1\n85: - Questions answered: Q1\n86: \n87: ## Reflection\n88: \n89: - What worked and why: `git log -S` over both path spellings, then reading the full diff of the one commit that matched, converted a vague \"the reaper lost something\" into an exact deletion with a date and a reason. Re-measuring the live process census in the same session kept the fourteen-day-old daemon observation current instead of inherited.\n90: - What did not work and why: chasing config provenance through git history was structurally impossible — the key is unversioned. Reading the wrapper's write-back found the real persistence mechanism instead.\n91: - What I would do differently: start from the code path that writes the key (write-back) rather than from \"who unset it\", because an unset that is silently re-pinned is a different bug from an unset that stays unset.\n92: \n93: ## Recommended Next Focus\n94: \n95: Q2 and Q3 together: git 2.50.1's fsmonitor daemon lifecycle on macOS — whether a daemon can exit on its own, what `fsmonitor.socketDir`, idle shutdown and `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` do, and whether `--ipc-threads=8` or the cookie handshake has a known wedge in this version. The `--ipc-threads=8` argument in every live process is not something this repo passes, so its origin and its deadlock surface need an authoritative answer."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 100, \"path\": \"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-001.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793094541412,"end":1793094542347}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793094542371,"sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","part":{"partID":"prt_17cc35a300015PU1Py1gDH3N4x","sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","messageID":"msg_17cc31d860015uQUz3uSRnhckJ","type":"tool","id":"call_2b22709d93faaa0f96a4b2ba","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd \"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017\" && echo \"=== iter-001.jsonl full (first 6 lines) ===\" && head -6 deltas/iter-001.jsonl | cut -c1-600 && echo \"\" && echo \"=== prompts/iter-001.event.json ===\" && cat prompts/iter-001.event.json | cut -c1-800 && echo \"\" && echo \"=== synthesis-events dir ===\" && ls -la prompts/synthesis-events/ && echo \"=== logs dir ===\" && ls logs/ 2>/dev/null && echo \"=== ledger ===\" && ls deep-research-ledger/ 2>/dev/null && ls deep-research-ledger/frames/ | head -5"},"output":"=== iter-001.jsonl full (first 6 lines) ===\n{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":1,\"run\":1,\"runId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"sessionId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"lineageId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"newInfoRatio\":0.85,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"The sk-git/026 regression: who moved the worktrees back and where the reaper's daemon handling went\",\"findingsCount\":10,\"noveltyJustification\":\"Nine of ten findings \n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-001\",\"label\":\"Commit 3c7c3f83874 (2026-09-03) deleted the reaper's --reap-daemons flag, the _daemon_worktree_path_from_cmdline and _daemon_cmdline_is_orphan helpers, and the whole ORPHAN DAEMON REPORTING block from .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\"git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-002\",\"label\":\"The deleted reaper logic only enumerated pgrep -f context-server.js and skipped any command line not matching *node*context-server.js*, so it targeted retired spec-memory MCP daemons and never matched git fsmonitor--daemon.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\"git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\",\".skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-003\",\"label\":\"Restoring the reaper's daemon handling would not stop a single fsmonitor daemon; a fsmonitor reaper is new work rather than a revert.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\".skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\",\".skilled/skills/sk-git/feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md:85\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-004\",\"label\":\"speckit.worktreeBase is unversioned local config: no commit can unset it, and this checkout currently has no such key set, so the relocation policy record simply is not in the repository.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\"git config --show-origin --get-regexp fsmonitor|untracked|worktreeBase|extensions\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-005\",\"label\":\".skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292-295 writes speckit.worktreeBase whenever the key is unset, and _wn_base_dir() defaults to <toplevel>/.worktrees, so one launch without SPECKIT_WORKTREE_BASE re-pins the in-checkout base permanently.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\".skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292\",\".skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:71\"]}\n\n=== prompts/iter-001.event.json ===\n{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":1,\"run\":1,\"runId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"sessionId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"lineageId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"newInfoRatio\":0.85,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"The sk-git/026 regression: who moved the worktrees back and where the reaper's daemon handling went\",\"findingsCount\":10,\"noveltyJustification\":\"Nine of ten findings are new to the packet: the deletion commit, the MCP-versus-fsmonitor family correction, the wrapper config write-back, and the live census were all unrecorded.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Q1. What unset speckit\n\n=== synthesis-events dir ===\ntotal 8\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 3 michelkerkmeester  staff   96 Oct 27 09:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 6 michelkerkmeester  staff  192 Oct 27 09:27 ..\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 michelkerkmeester  staff  223 Oct 27 09:27 synthesis_complete.json\n=== logs dir ===\nfanout-lineage.err\nfanout-lineage.out\n=== ledger ===\nframes\npending\nquarantine\nrecoveries\nstale-locks\n0000000000000001.frame\n0000000000000002.frame\n0000000000000003.frame\n0000000000000004.frame\n0000000000000005.frame\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== iter-001.jsonl full (first 6 lines) ===\n{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":1,\"run\":1,\"runId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"sessionId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"lineageId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"newInfoRatio\":0.85,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"The sk-git/026 regression: who moved the worktrees back and where the reaper's daemon handling went\",\"findingsCount\":10,\"noveltyJustification\":\"Nine of ten findings \n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-001\",\"label\":\"Commit 3c7c3f83874 (2026-09-03) deleted the reaper's --reap-daemons flag, the _daemon_worktree_path_from_cmdline and _daemon_cmdline_is_orphan helpers, and the whole ORPHAN DAEMON REPORTING block from .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\"git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-002\",\"label\":\"The deleted reaper logic only enumerated pgrep -f context-server.js and skipped any command line not matching *node*context-server.js*, so it targeted retired spec-memory MCP daemons and never matched git fsmonitor--daemon.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\"git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\",\".skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-003\",\"label\":\"Restoring the reaper's daemon handling would not stop a single fsmonitor daemon; a fsmonitor reaper is new work rather than a revert.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\".skilled/bin/worktree-reaper.sh\",\".skilled/skills/sk-git/feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md:85\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-004\",\"label\":\"speckit.worktreeBase is unversioned local config: no commit can unset it, and this checkout currently has no such key set, so the relocation policy record simply is not in the repository.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\"git config --show-origin --get-regexp fsmonitor|untracked|worktreeBase|extensions\"]}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"f-iter001-005\",\"label\":\".skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292-295 writes speckit.worktreeBase whenever the key is unset, and _wn_base_dir() defaults to <toplevel>/.worktrees, so one launch without SPECKIT_WORKTREE_BASE re-pins the in-checkout base permanently.\",\"iteration\":1,\"sources\":[\".skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292\",\".skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:71\"]}\n\n=== prompts/iter-001.event.json ===\n{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":1,\"run\":1,\"runId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"sessionId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"lineageId\":\"fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169\",\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"newInfoRatio\":0.85,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"The sk-git/026 regression: who moved the worktrees back and where the reaper's daemon handling went\",\"findingsCount\":10,\"noveltyJustification\":\"Nine of ten findings are new to the packet: the deletion commit, the MCP-versus-fsmonitor family correction, the wrapper config write-back, and the live census were all unrecorded.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Q1. What unset speckit\n\n=== synthesis-events dir ===\ntotal 8\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 3 michelkerkmeester  staff   96 Oct 27 09:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 6 michelkerkmeester  staff  192 Oct 27 09:27 ..\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 michelkerkmeester  staff  223 Oct 27 09:27 synthesis_complete.json\n=== logs dir ===\nfanout-lineage.err\nfanout-lineage.out\n=== ledger ===\nframes\npending\nquarantine\nrecoveries\nstale-locks\n0000000000000001.frame\n0000000000000002.frame\n0000000000000003.frame\n0000000000000004.frame\n0000000000000005.frame\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd \\\"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017\\\" && echo \\\"=== iter-001.jsonl full (first 6 lines) ===\\\" && head -6 deltas/iter-001.jsonl | cut -c1-600 && echo \\\"\\\" && echo \\\"=== prompts/iter-001.event.json ===\\\" && cat prompts/iter-001.event.json | cut -c1-800 && echo \\\"\\\" && echo \\\"=== synthesis-events dir ===\\\" && ls -la prompts/synthesis-events/ && echo \\\"=== logs dir ===\\\" && ls logs/ 2>/dev/null && echo \\\"=== ledger ===\\\" && ls deep-research-ledger/ 2>/dev/null && ls deep-research-ledger/frames/ | head -5\"}"},"time":{"start":1793094539824,"end":1793094542371}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793094543667,"sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","part":{"id":"prt_17cc36933001Cwezi114iL5FAp","sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","messageID":"msg_17cc31d860015uQUz3uSRnhckJ","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","cost":0.0010771600000000002,"tokens":{"input":3399,"output":334,"reasoning":1075,"cache":{"read":125824,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793094545460,"sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","part":{"id":"prt_17cc37034001nvkZPmJHGjt64G","sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","messageID":"msg_17cc3693a001olKqFu2Vi1QpQM","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793094556830,"sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","part":{"partID":"prt_17cc399b40011gpBHBNY0lSajZ","sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","messageID":"msg_17cc3693a001olKqFu2Vi1QpQM","type":"tool","id":"call_7bcf52a1a5319c2be57e8cec","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":120,"path":"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/research.md"},"output":"Read file specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/research.md, lines 1-120\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Research: git fsmonitor daemon pile-up and the 30-minute pre-push stall\"\n3: description: \"Root cause and fix design for the fsmonitor IPC stall in this repo, the eleven-daemon pile-up across nested worktrees, and the sk-git/026 relocation regression.\"\n4: importance_tier: important\n5: contextType: research\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Research: git fsmonitor daemon pile-up and the 30-minute pre-push stall\n9: \n10: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: research-report | v1.0 -->\n11: \n12: **Packet**: `specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017`\n13: **Run**: `fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169`, iteration lineage of the fsmonitor research fan-out\n14: **Iterations**: 3 (lineage cap)\n15: **Stop reason**: `maxIterationsReached`\n16: **Questions**: 5 of 5 answered\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. Summary\n21: \n22: The 30-minute push was not a slow hook. It was an unbounded wait inside a synchronisation protocol that git itself documents as infinite on both sides.\n23: \n24: A `git check-ignore` call in the pre-push skill gate asked the fsmonitor daemon what had changed. The daemon answers that question by creating a cookie file inside the watched tree and blocking until its filesystem-event thread reports seeing that file. If the event never arrives, the daemon's worker thread waits forever — the source comment calls this \"an infinite wait\" and says the timed variant was never implemented. The client that asked (`read_packetized_to_strbuf`) has no deadline either, so it waits forever too. The only release observed in practice was killing the daemon, which closes the socket.\n25: \n26: Three conditions made that latent defect a 30-minute outage:\n27: \n28: 1. `core.fsmonitor=true` is shared config, so every worktree and the main checkout each start their own daemon on demand.\n29: 2. The worktrees had moved back inside the main checkout, so the main daemon watched roughly 2M extra files across 93 `node_modules` trees and received an event for every file any worktree touched.\n30: 3. On macOS the daemon has no idle exit and its health thread is an empty stub, so daemons are immortal, are never recycled, and are never replaced when wedged. Eleven were alive, the oldest for 16 days.\n31: \n32: The hook had no ceiling, and its fallback only handles a returned failure, so a hang had no exit. That is the whole causal chain, and each link is separately fixable.\n33: \n34: The packet's framing of one link needs correction: the reaper's removed daemon handling targeted retired MCP `context-server.js` daemons, not git fsmonitor daemons. Restoring it would not stop a single fsmonitor daemon. A fsmonitor reaper is new work.\n35: \n36: ---\n37: \n38: ## 2. Symptom and evidence timeline\n39: \n40: | Time (2026-10-27) | Observation |\n41: |---|---|\n42: | ~08:00 | `git push origin main` enters the pre-push hook; total stall about 30 minutes; push then lands (`e023536201..f273dba74c`). |\n43: | — | Child `git check-ignore -z --stdin` (PID 28423) sleeps for 16+ minutes at 0 % CPU. |\n44: | — | `sample 28423` stack: `repo_read_index → read_index_from → tweak_fsmonitor → refresh_fsmonitor → fsmonitor_ipc__send_query → ipc_client_send_command_to_connection → read_packetized_to_strbuf`. |\n45: | 08:34 | Main-checkout daemon PID 23895 holds `.git/fsmonitor--daemon.ipc`. `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` hangs and is killed after 15 s. |\n46: | 08:51:33 | `kill 23895` releases the blocked `check-ignore`; git auto-starts PID 17228. |\n47: | after restart | Each `git check-ignore` takes about 10 s, against a normal fraction of a second. |\n48: | 09:08:10 | Another worktree daemon starts, taking the population to 12. |\n49: | 09:17:59 | Daemon 17228 is gone; PID 18482 takes the socket. A blocked `git status` returns; its step logs `duration_ms: 189183`. |\n50: | 09:22Z | Lineage census: 11 `fsmonitor--daemon run --detach --ipc-threads=8` processes, oldest PID 1157 from Oct 11 18:29:55. |\n51: \n52: A fresh daemon wedged within about 23 minutes, so the stall is not an artefact of one long-lived process. Restarting is not a fix.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. Method\n57: \n58: Three iterations, each with one focus:\n59: \n60: 1. **The regression (Q1).** Read the completed sk-git/026 packet, traced the reaper with `git log -S daemon` and read the full diff of the commit that dropped its daemon block, traced the worktree-base write-back, and re-measured the live worktree and daemon population.\n61: 2. **The daemon (Q2, Q3).** Read the installed build's manual (`Git 2.50.1.428.g0e8243`) and the exact v2.50.1 sources that define the lifecycle, the cookie handshake and the IPC client read, then checked 2.51-2.53 release notes for any announced fix.\n62: 3. **The repo (Q4, Q5).** Followed the hook symlink chain into the gate script and its imported manifest builder, enumerated unbounded git call sites, found the repository's existing fsmonitor opt-out convention, and derived the default posture and reaping design.\n63: \n64: Read-only discipline: every git command used is non-index-reading (`log`, `show`, `config`, `rev-parse`), because an index-reading command in this checkout is exactly the failure under study. Status-time measurement was deliberately not performed: it can write the index, which is a repository write outside this lineage's authority.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 4. Root-cause chain\n69: \n70: Five links, in order of causation:\n71: \n72: **4.1 Config: fsmonitor on everywhere.** `core.fsmonitor=true` and `core.untrackedCache=true` live in the shared `.git/config`, so they apply to the main checkout and, through the shared config, to every worktree. Each working directory starts its own daemon on first index read. [SOURCE: git config --show-origin --get-regexp, iteration 1]\n73: \n74: **4.2 Topology: worktrees inside the checkout.** The relocation to `~/worktrees/public` recorded by sk-git/026 had been undone: the config key is unset, `~/worktrees` is empty, and 13 worktrees sit under `Public/.worktrees/`. The main daemon therefore watches roughly 570k files plus roughly 2M under `.worktrees/`, including 93 `node_modules` trees, and every worktree file is watched twice — once by the main daemon and once by its own. [SOURCE: ls -d .worktrees/*, this session] [SOURCE: specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/scratch/findings.md]\n75: \n76: **4.3 Lifecycle: daemons never retire.** On macOS the daemon has no idle timeout, no TTL, and a health thread that returns immediately, so it cannot detect its own stall or end itself. Nothing in the repo stops a daemon when its worktree goes away; `worktree-reaper.sh` is manual and now promises never to signal a process. Eleven daemons accumulated, one of them 16 days old. [SOURCE: compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health-darwin.c:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/README.md:168]\n77: \n78: **4.4 Protocol: unbounded wait on both sides.** The daemon blocks in `while (cookie->result == FCIR_INIT) pthread_cond_wait(...)` after writing a cookie into the watched tree; the source comment admits this is an infinite wait. The client blocks in `read_packetized_to_strbuf` with no poll and no timeout. One undelivered filesystem event for the cookie therefore parks one daemon worker and one client forever, and eight such clients saturate the pool so that `status` and `stop` stop answering too. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:198] [SOURCE: compat/simple-ipc/ipc-unix-socket.c:192]\n79: \n80: **4.5 Repo: the hook has no ceiling.** `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:129` runs `spawnSync('git', ['check-ignore', '-z', '--stdin'], { cwd, input, encoding })` with no `timeout` and no `-c core.fsmonitor=false`, once per skill root, reached from `ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs` in the pre-push gate. The fallback at line 136 needs a returned result, so it cannot rescue a hang. A hang is not a failure; nothing above it imposes a ceiling. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:129] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:51]\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 5. Answers to the five questions\n85: \n86: ### Q1. What unset `speckit.worktreeBase` and removed the reaper's daemon handling, and does anything still create worktrees under `.worktrees/` by default?\n87: \n88: **Two unrelated events, and yes.**\n89: \n90: - The reaper's daemon block was deleted by `3c7c3f83874` (2026-09-03, \"refactor(runtime): remove the memory launcher, plugin, hooks and the tool-ownership commit gate\"), not by the `.opencode` → `.skilled` source-root move. That commit removed `--reap-daemons`, `_daemon_worktree_path_from_cmdline()`, `_daemon_cmdline_is_orphan()` and the whole \"ORPHAN DAEMON REPORTING\" block. [SOURCE: git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh]\n91: - The removed logic only matched `pgrep -f 'context-server\\.js'` and skipped any other command line, i.e. retired spec-memory MCP daemons. It never matched `git fsmonitor--daemon`. Restoring it would reap nothing relevant.\n92: - The base key is unversioned local config, so no commit unset it. The durable in-checkout default is re-created by `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292-295`, which writes the resolved base into `.git/config` whenever the key is unset, while `_wn_base_dir()` defaults to `<toplevel>/.worktrees`. One launch without `SPECKIT_WORKTREE_BASE` silently and permanently re-pins the in-checkout layout. That write-back arrived in `f83952c3a94` (2026-09-11), three weeks after the base became configurable in `a69a621a39a` (2026-08-21). [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:71]\n93: - `.worktrees` remains the documented code default (`worktree-naming.sh:13`, `README.md:115,125`), and the changelog advertises unsetting the config as the supported way back to it.\n94: - **UNKNOWN**: which specific action unset the key. No shell-history hit; `.git/config` is not versioned. Resolving check: a `.git/config` copy in a backup, or shell history from the session that ran the unset.\n95: \n96: ### Q2. Git 2.50.1's documented daemon lifecycle on macOS, `fsmonitor.socketDir`, and `stop` on a wedged daemon\n97: \n98: **There is no idle shutdown and no self-exit.**\n99: \n100: - The manual documents `start`, `run`, `stop`, `status` over a single working directory, and describes the daemon only as \"long running\". No idle timeout, no lifetime bound, no TTL. [SOURCE: man git-fsmonitor--daemon]\n101: - `fsmonitor.socketDir` only relocates the Unix socket when `.git` (or `$HOME`) is on a network mount; it has no bearing on lifecycle.\n102: - On macOS the health thread is an empty stub: `fsm_health__ctor`, `fsm_health__loop` and `fsm_health__stop_async` all return immediately, so the daemon never monitors its listener and never exits on health grounds. [SOURCE: compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health-darwin.c:1]\n103: - `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` sends `quit` and then polls `fsmonitor_ipc__get_state()` every 50 ms with no deadline, so on a wedged daemon the stop command hangs — the observed 15-second kill. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:88]\n104: - `fsmonitor.starttimeout` (60 s default) is the only timeout in the lifecycle and bounds startup alone. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:38]\n105: \n106: ### Q3. Event overload, or a known daemon deadlock?\n107: \n108: **The defect is an acknowledged unbounded wait; overload is the trigger.**\n109: \n110: - Daemon side: every non-`quit` query creates a cookie file in the watched tree and blocks on `pthread_cond_wait` until the event thread reports the cookie seen. The comment states it is an infinite wait and that `pthread_cond_timedwait()` was wanted but unavailable. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:198]\n111: - Client side: `ipc_client_send_command_to_connection` writes the command then calls `read_packetized_to_strbuf` with no poll and no deadline; only EOF or a read error unblocks it. [SOURCE: compat/simple-ipc/ipc-unix-socket.c:192]\n112: - `--ipc-threads=8` is git's compiled default for `fsmonitor.ipcthreads`, not repo configuration, and this checkout sets no `fsmonitor.*` key. It multiplies the number of clients that can be parked, but it is not the cause. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:35]\n113: - No announced fix: the 2.51, 2.52 and 2.53 release notes contain zero fsmonitor entries. Upgrading is not the fix path. [SOURCE: Documentation/RelNotes/2.51.0.adoc]\n114: - A large event source is the plausible way to lose a cookie's event, which is why the topology link matters: the main daemon sits on top of 2.5M files it should never have been watching.\n115: \n116: ### Q4. Which call sites run git without a timeout, and which should opt out of fsmonitor?\n117: \n118: Proven hung site:\n119: \n120: | File | Call | Timeout | fsmonitor opt-out |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 121]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/research.md, lines 1-120\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Research: git fsmonitor daemon pile-up and the 30-minute pre-push stall\"\n3: description: \"Root cause and fix design for the fsmonitor IPC stall in this repo, the eleven-daemon pile-up across nested worktrees, and the sk-git/026 relocation regression.\"\n4: importance_tier: important\n5: contextType: research\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Research: git fsmonitor daemon pile-up and the 30-minute pre-push stall\n9: \n10: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: research-report | v1.0 -->\n11: \n12: **Packet**: `specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017`\n13: **Run**: `fanout-llmgateway-flash-1017-1793088864734-4s5169`, iteration lineage of the fsmonitor research fan-out\n14: **Iterations**: 3 (lineage cap)\n15: **Stop reason**: `maxIterationsReached`\n16: **Questions**: 5 of 5 answered\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. Summary\n21: \n22: The 30-minute push was not a slow hook. It was an unbounded wait inside a synchronisation protocol that git itself documents as infinite on both sides.\n23: \n24: A `git check-ignore` call in the pre-push skill gate asked the fsmonitor daemon what had changed. The daemon answers that question by creating a cookie file inside the watched tree and blocking until its filesystem-event thread reports seeing that file. If the event never arrives, the daemon's worker thread waits forever — the source comment calls this \"an infinite wait\" and says the timed variant was never implemented. The client that asked (`read_packetized_to_strbuf`) has no deadline either, so it waits forever too. The only release observed in practice was killing the daemon, which closes the socket.\n25: \n26: Three conditions made that latent defect a 30-minute outage:\n27: \n28: 1. `core.fsmonitor=true` is shared config, so every worktree and the main checkout each start their own daemon on demand.\n29: 2. The worktrees had moved back inside the main checkout, so the main daemon watched roughly 2M extra files across 93 `node_modules` trees and received an event for every file any worktree touched.\n30: 3. On macOS the daemon has no idle exit and its health thread is an empty stub, so daemons are immortal, are never recycled, and are never replaced when wedged. Eleven were alive, the oldest for 16 days.\n31: \n32: The hook had no ceiling, and its fallback only handles a returned failure, so a hang had no exit. That is the whole causal chain, and each link is separately fixable.\n33: \n34: The packet's framing of one link needs correction: the reaper's removed daemon handling targeted retired MCP `context-server.js` daemons, not git fsmonitor daemons. Restoring it would not stop a single fsmonitor daemon. A fsmonitor reaper is new work.\n35: \n36: ---\n37: \n38: ## 2. Symptom and evidence timeline\n39: \n40: | Time (2026-10-27) | Observation |\n41: |---|---|\n42: | ~08:00 | `git push origin main` enters the pre-push hook; total stall about 30 minutes; push then lands (`e023536201..f273dba74c`). |\n43: | — | Child `git check-ignore -z --stdin` (PID 28423) sleeps for 16+ minutes at 0 % CPU. |\n44: | — | `sample 28423` stack: `repo_read_index → read_index_from → tweak_fsmonitor → refresh_fsmonitor → fsmonitor_ipc__send_query → ipc_client_send_command_to_connection → read_packetized_to_strbuf`. |\n45: | 08:34 | Main-checkout daemon PID 23895 holds `.git/fsmonitor--daemon.ipc`. `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` hangs and is killed after 15 s. |\n46: | 08:51:33 | `kill 23895` releases the blocked `check-ignore`; git auto-starts PID 17228. |\n47: | after restart | Each `git check-ignore` takes about 10 s, against a normal fraction of a second. |\n48: | 09:08:10 | Another worktree daemon starts, taking the population to 12. |\n49: | 09:17:59 | Daemon 17228 is gone; PID 18482 takes the socket. A blocked `git status` returns; its step logs `duration_ms: 189183`. |\n50: | 09:22Z | Lineage census: 11 `fsmonitor--daemon run --detach --ipc-threads=8` processes, oldest PID 1157 from Oct 11 18:29:55. |\n51: \n52: A fresh daemon wedged within about 23 minutes, so the stall is not an artefact of one long-lived process. Restarting is not a fix.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. Method\n57: \n58: Three iterations, each with one focus:\n59: \n60: 1. **The regression (Q1).** Read the completed sk-git/026 packet, traced the reaper with `git log -S daemon` and read the full diff of the commit that dropped its daemon block, traced the worktree-base write-back, and re-measured the live worktree and daemon population.\n61: 2. **The daemon (Q2, Q3).** Read the installed build's manual (`Git 2.50.1.428.g0e8243`) and the exact v2.50.1 sources that define the lifecycle, the cookie handshake and the IPC client read, then checked 2.51-2.53 release notes for any announced fix.\n62: 3. **The repo (Q4, Q5).** Followed the hook symlink chain into the gate script and its imported manifest builder, enumerated unbounded git call sites, found the repository's existing fsmonitor opt-out convention, and derived the default posture and reaping design.\n63: \n64: Read-only discipline: every git command used is non-index-reading (`log`, `show`, `config`, `rev-parse`), because an index-reading command in this checkout is exactly the failure under study. Status-time measurement was deliberately not performed: it can write the index, which is a repository write outside this lineage's authority.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 4. Root-cause chain\n69: \n70: Five links, in order of causation:\n71: \n72: **4.1 Config: fsmonitor on everywhere.** `core.fsmonitor=true` and `core.untrackedCache=true` live in the shared `.git/config`, so they apply to the main checkout and, through the shared config, to every worktree. Each working directory starts its own daemon on first index read. [SOURCE: git config --show-origin --get-regexp, iteration 1]\n73: \n74: **4.2 Topology: worktrees inside the checkout.** The relocation to `~/worktrees/public` recorded by sk-git/026 had been undone: the config key is unset, `~/worktrees` is empty, and 13 worktrees sit under `Public/.worktrees/`. The main daemon therefore watches roughly 570k files plus roughly 2M under `.worktrees/`, including 93 `node_modules` trees, and every worktree file is watched twice — once by the main daemon and once by its own. [SOURCE: ls -d .worktrees/*, this session] [SOURCE: specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/scratch/findings.md]\n75: \n76: **4.3 Lifecycle: daemons never retire.** On macOS the daemon has no idle timeout, no TTL, and a health thread that returns immediately, so it cannot detect its own stall or end itself. Nothing in the repo stops a daemon when its worktree goes away; `worktree-reaper.sh` is manual and now promises never to signal a process. Eleven daemons accumulated, one of them 16 days old. [SOURCE: compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health-darwin.c:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/README.md:168]\n77: \n78: **4.4 Protocol: unbounded wait on both sides.** The daemon blocks in `while (cookie->result == FCIR_INIT) pthread_cond_wait(...)` after writing a cookie into the watched tree; the source comment admits this is an infinite wait. The client blocks in `read_packetized_to_strbuf` with no poll and no timeout. One undelivered filesystem event for the cookie therefore parks one daemon worker and one client forever, and eight such clients saturate the pool so that `status` and `stop` stop answering too. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:198] [SOURCE: compat/simple-ipc/ipc-unix-socket.c:192]\n79: \n80: **4.5 Repo: the hook has no ceiling.** `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:129` runs `spawnSync('git', ['check-ignore', '-z', '--stdin'], { cwd, input, encoding })` with no `timeout` and no `-c core.fsmonitor=false`, once per skill root, reached from `ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs` in the pre-push gate. The fallback at line 136 needs a returned result, so it cannot rescue a hang. A hang is not a failure; nothing above it imposes a ceiling. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:129] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:51]\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 5. Answers to the five questions\n85: \n86: ### Q1. What unset `speckit.worktreeBase` and removed the reaper's daemon handling, and does anything still create worktrees under `.worktrees/` by default?\n87: \n88: **Two unrelated events, and yes.**\n89: \n90: - The reaper's daemon block was deleted by `3c7c3f83874` (2026-09-03, \"refactor(runtime): remove the memory launcher, plugin, hooks and the tool-ownership commit gate\"), not by the `.opencode` → `.skilled` source-root move. That commit removed `--reap-daemons`, `_daemon_worktree_path_from_cmdline()`, `_daemon_cmdline_is_orphan()` and the whole \"ORPHAN DAEMON REPORTING\" block. [SOURCE: git show 3c7c3f83874 -- .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh]\n91: - The removed logic only matched `pgrep -f 'context-server\\.js'` and skipped any other command line, i.e. retired spec-memory MCP daemons. It never matched `git fsmonitor--daemon`. Restoring it would reap nothing relevant.\n92: - The base key is unversioned local config, so no commit unset it. The durable in-checkout default is re-created by `.skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292-295`, which writes the resolved base into `.git/config` whenever the key is unset, while `_wn_base_dir()` defaults to `<toplevel>/.worktrees`. One launch without `SPECKIT_WORKTREE_BASE` silently and permanently re-pins the in-checkout layout. That write-back arrived in `f83952c3a94` (2026-09-11), three weeks after the base became configurable in `a69a621a39a` (2026-08-21). [SOURCE: .skilled/bin/worktree-session.sh:292] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh:71]\n93: - `.worktrees` remains the documented code default (`worktree-naming.sh:13`, `README.md:115,125`), and the changelog advertises unsetting the config as the supported way back to it.\n94: - **UNKNOWN**: which specific action unset the key. No shell-history hit; `.git/config` is not versioned. Resolving check: a `.git/config` copy in a backup, or shell history from the session that ran the unset.\n95: \n96: ### Q2. Git 2.50.1's documented daemon lifecycle on macOS, `fsmonitor.socketDir`, and `stop` on a wedged daemon\n97: \n98: **There is no idle shutdown and no self-exit.**\n99: \n100: - The manual documents `start`, `run`, `stop`, `status` over a single working directory, and describes the daemon only as \"long running\". No idle timeout, no lifetime bound, no TTL. [SOURCE: man git-fsmonitor--daemon]\n101: - `fsmonitor.socketDir` only relocates the Unix socket when `.git` (or `$HOME`) is on a network mount; it has no bearing on lifecycle.\n102: - On macOS the health thread is an empty stub: `fsm_health__ctor`, `fsm_health__loop` and `fsm_health__stop_async` all return immediately, so the daemon never monitors its listener and never exits on health grounds. [SOURCE: compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health-darwin.c:1]\n103: - `git fsmonitor--daemon stop` sends `quit` and then polls `fsmonitor_ipc__get_state()` every 50 ms with no deadline, so on a wedged daemon the stop command hangs — the observed 15-second kill. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:88]\n104: - `fsmonitor.starttimeout` (60 s default) is the only timeout in the lifecycle and bounds startup alone. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:38]\n105: \n106: ### Q3. Event overload, or a known daemon deadlock?\n107: \n108: **The defect is an acknowledged unbounded wait; overload is the trigger.**\n109: \n110: - Daemon side: every non-`quit` query creates a cookie file in the watched tree and blocks on `pthread_cond_wait` until the event thread reports the cookie seen. The comment states it is an infinite wait and that `pthread_cond_timedwait()` was wanted but unavailable. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:198]\n111: - Client side: `ipc_client_send_command_to_connection` writes the command then calls `read_packetized_to_strbuf` with no poll and no deadline; only EOF or a read error unblocks it. [SOURCE: compat/simple-ipc/ipc-unix-socket.c:192]\n112: - `--ipc-threads=8` is git's compiled default for `fsmonitor.ipcthreads`, not repo configuration, and this checkout sets no `fsmonitor.*` key. It multiplies the number of clients that can be parked, but it is not the cause. [SOURCE: builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c:35]\n113: - No announced fix: the 2.51, 2.52 and 2.53 release notes contain zero fsmonitor entries. Upgrading is not the fix path. [SOURCE: Documentation/RelNotes/2.51.0.adoc]\n114: - A large event source is the plausible way to lose a cookie's event, which is why the topology link matters: the main daemon sits on top of 2.5M files it should never have been watching.\n115: \n116: ### Q4. Which call sites run git without a timeout, and which should opt out of fsmonitor?\n117: \n118: Proven hung site:\n119: \n120: | File | Call | Timeout | fsmonitor opt-out |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 121]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 120, \"path\": \"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/research.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793094556084,"end":1793094556830}}}}
