{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793094712453,"sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","part":{"partID":"prt_17cc5fb5f001tzQYgMKnJtbEFh","sessionID":"ses_e833fbb1effewOnhN31WYdpXxT","messageID":"msg_17cc5c852001ygeEh5Ipwxdlrj","type":"tool","id":"call_a6d094e5806a566ce8983e62","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":80,"offset":60,"path":"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-002.md"},"output":"Read file specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-002.md, lines 60-93\n60: ## Dead Ends\n61: \n62: - Locating a Darwin health-check policy: the implementation is an empty stub, so there is no policy to read. The daemon's own liveness is explicitly unmonitored on macOS.\n63: \n64: ## Edge Cases\n65: \n66: - Missing dependency: the exact reason PID 17228 disappeared at 09:17:59. The source rules out a timeout; a signal or crash remains. The resolving check is the unified log or `~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports` entry for that PID, which this lineage did not read.\n67: - Contradictory evidence: the packet's section 4 INFERENCE says event volume \"overloaded the main daemon until it stopped answering\", while the source shows the stall is an unbounded wait. Both are compatible once overload is read as the trigger; recorded as findings 8-9 rather than collapsed.\n68: \n69: ## Sources Consulted\n70: \n71: - `man git-fsmonitor--daemon` (Git 2.50.1.428.g0e8243)\n72: - `man git-config` — `core.fsmonitor`, `fsmonitor.socketDir`\n73: - `builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c` (git v2.50.1): lines 35-61, 88-101, 198-212, 689-772, 1154-1275, 1510\n74: - `compat/simple-ipc/ipc-unix-socket.c` (git v2.50.1): lines 192-220, 440-506\n75: - `compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health-darwin.c`, `compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health.h` (git v2.50.1)\n76: - `Documentation/RelNotes/2.51.0.adoc`, `2.52.0.adoc`, `2.53.0.adoc` (git v2.51.0, v2.52.0, v2.53.0)\n77: - `specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/scratch/findings.md`\n78: \n79: ## Assessment\n80: \n81: - New information ratio: 0.90\n82: - Questions addressed: Q2, Q3\n83: - Questions answered: Q2, Q3\n84: \n85: ## Reflection\n86: \n87: - What worked and why: reading the installed build's man page first pinned the exact version, then reading that version's source turned \"the daemon stopped answering\" into named waits at named lines. The source comment admitting the infinite wait is stronger evidence than any inference about overload.\n88: - What did not work and why: my first guesses at source paths (`simple-ipc/`, `compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health.c`, `Documentation/RelNotes/*.txt`) all 404'd; the tree uses `compat/simple-ipc/` and `.adoc` release notes. Three path probes were wasted before checking status codes instead of reading empty output as \"no match\".\n89: - What I would do differently: probe HTTP status before interpreting a grep miss as evidence of absence — an empty grep on a 404 is not a finding.\n90: \n91: ## Recommended Next Focus\n92: \n93: Q4 and Q5 together: inventory every git call site in the repo's hooks and scripts that can block on the fsmonitor IPC (with `generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:129` as the proven one), decide which need a timeout versus `-c core.fsmonitor=false`, and turn the lifecycle facts into a default posture plus a daemon-reaping design that does not contradict the reaper's \"never signals a process\" invariant.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-002.md, lines 60-93\n60: ## Dead Ends\n61: \n62: - Locating a Darwin health-check policy: the implementation is an empty stub, so there is no policy to read. The daemon's own liveness is explicitly unmonitored on macOS.\n63: \n64: ## Edge Cases\n65: \n66: - Missing dependency: the exact reason PID 17228 disappeared at 09:17:59. The source rules out a timeout; a signal or crash remains. The resolving check is the unified log or `~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports` entry for that PID, which this lineage did not read.\n67: - Contradictory evidence: the packet's section 4 INFERENCE says event volume \"overloaded the main daemon until it stopped answering\", while the source shows the stall is an unbounded wait. Both are compatible once overload is read as the trigger; recorded as findings 8-9 rather than collapsed.\n68: \n69: ## Sources Consulted\n70: \n71: - `man git-fsmonitor--daemon` (Git 2.50.1.428.g0e8243)\n72: - `man git-config` — `core.fsmonitor`, `fsmonitor.socketDir`\n73: - `builtin/fsmonitor--daemon.c` (git v2.50.1): lines 35-61, 88-101, 198-212, 689-772, 1154-1275, 1510\n74: - `compat/simple-ipc/ipc-unix-socket.c` (git v2.50.1): lines 192-220, 440-506\n75: - `compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health-darwin.c`, `compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health.h` (git v2.50.1)\n76: - `Documentation/RelNotes/2.51.0.adoc`, `2.52.0.adoc`, `2.53.0.adoc` (git v2.51.0, v2.52.0, v2.53.0)\n77: - `specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/scratch/findings.md`\n78: \n79: ## Assessment\n80: \n81: - New information ratio: 0.90\n82: - Questions addressed: Q2, Q3\n83: - Questions answered: Q2, Q3\n84: \n85: ## Reflection\n86: \n87: - What worked and why: reading the installed build's man page first pinned the exact version, then reading that version's source turned \"the daemon stopped answering\" into named waits at named lines. The source comment admitting the infinite wait is stronger evidence than any inference about overload.\n88: - What did not work and why: my first guesses at source paths (`simple-ipc/`, `compat/fsmonitor/fsm-health.c`, `Documentation/RelNotes/*.txt`) all 404'd; the tree uses `compat/simple-ipc/` and `.adoc` release notes. Three path probes were wasted before checking status codes instead of reading empty output as \"no match\".\n89: - What I would do differently: probe HTTP status before interpreting a grep miss as evidence of absence — an empty grep on a 404 is not a finding.\n90: \n91: ## Recommended Next Focus\n92: \n93: Q4 and Q5 together: inventory every git call site in the repo's hooks and scripts that can block on the fsmonitor IPC (with `generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:129` as the proven one), decide which need a timeout versus `-c core.fsmonitor=false`, and turn the lifecycle facts into a default posture plus a daemon-reaping design that does not contradict the reaper's \"never signals a process\" invariant."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 80, \"offset\": 60, \"path\": \"specs/sk-git/035-fsmonitor-daemon-and-worktree-relocation-regression/001-deep-research/research/lineages/llmgateway-flash-1017/iterations/iteration-002.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793094712159,"end":1793094712453}}}}
